4hUd_ReVnu8YtU7CQ9QhXgƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;UIZixCG4qcTr6JOwhEV1je6hAFaq7V3K1G3Kh_aFzY8ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx2026-07-20T13:56:28.916Zƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;iOQhnqm0oVocAIl-493qtlbNqi5-Q9A2Omt7TZqx90gƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;hDuQLsGyp6fcvf7qPn4DKBs4Z0Xbtu9h2HggNJ6G34sƒdpairƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;1Kjln82ezosfaqhVdScwW3CKYpBD94ARCFeyTgXd-dsƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkrCarbon Brief Staffƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;fRgGqDIsbFQCZrHA_GEyI2k039RtbH7LlWgKC5_1NOIƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx$https://www.carbonbrief.org/?p=63538ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;BLrIHIr83eCZ25yjA2PP603Ukzj5KmSvksl7t4YC70Mƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkxWed, 15 Jul 2026 14:49:04 +0000ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;nKH3DtjJHEXnDa5x2VXNaNmsXgi1IP1w3gr5RcbP5f4ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkcrssƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;ynNEKe4GYDxXl6B99B19naLuVy-g_xJMJGyfUj6jL18ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarky.ºWe handpick and explain the most important stories at the intersection of climate, land, food and nature over the past fortnight. This is an online version of Carbon Briefâ€™s fortnightly Cropped email newsletter. Subscribe for&nbsp; free here. Key developments Global drought and heat&nbsp; DRY THEN WET: A recent heatwave and months of low rainfall has led to a prolonged drought for Uganda, resulting in at least 16 deaths from hunger and significant crop losses, reported BBC News . Bastille Post Global suggested that â€œa developing El NiÃ±o later this year could bring heavier rainfall to parts of the region, raising the risk of flooding in areas now struggling with droughtâ€.&nbsp; FUNDING FOOD: The UN Food and Agriculture Organization (FAO) and the World Food Programme (WFP) have appealed for $200m in funding to help African nations deal with the impact of El NiÃ±o, stated Deutsche Welle . This would target 22 high-risk countries with measures, including â€œcash transfers, climate-resilient seeds, livestock protection and flood control.â€ The Guardian explained how El NiÃ±o could still â€œcause a severe shock to global food prices lasting into 2028â€.&nbsp; FARMING FEARS: Extreme weather has devastated agriculture across the world. India saw its driest June in 12 years, reported BBC News , and France has had a â€œdouble-digit productionâ€ decline, according to Le Monde . The Financial Times reported that farmers in the UK are mitigating the impacts of extreme heat by eliminating â€œchemicals and intensive ploughing to improve soil quality so it retains waterâ€. EURO FIRES: Wildfires have spread across Europe, with Spain reporting at least 12 deaths so far, according to the Guardian , and France experiencing road closures, said Reuters . Wildfire Today reported that the most extreme conditions are â€œacross France, Spain and northern Portugal, the Alpine arc extending into northern Italy, the south of the UK and south-east Irelandâ€. CNN explained how â€œthe climate crisis is driving hotter, drier weather, which is setting the stage for fiercer fire seasonsâ€. Endangering species&nbsp; REDEFINING HARM: The Trump administration â€œreversed decades of longstanding environmental law protecting endangered speciesâ€¦opening up sensitive habitatsâ€¦to drilling, mining, farming and real estate developmentâ€, reported CNN .&nbsp; According to the story, the change â€œredefines what constitutes â€˜harmâ€™â€ to endangered species, which historically prohibited habitat modification or degradation. Agence France-Presse reported that US environmental groups sued the Trump government over the move, arguing that it had violated â€œcommon sense, biological science and federal lawâ€.&nbsp; OPEN SEASON: Reuters reported that the change â€œlimits the reach of the 50-year-old Endangered Species Actâ€ (ESA), which is a â€œkey regulatory considerationâ€ when granting permits for â€œoil and gas, mining, electric transmission and â€‹other operations on federal lands and waterâ€. Legal scholars told the New York Times the US government â€œwas acting without conducting scientific research into the impactâ€ of the change, while the National Mining Association â€œapplauded the announcementâ€.&nbsp; News and views INTERNATIONAL WATERS: After a significant delay , the UK ratified the Biodiversity Beyond National Jurisdiction Agreement (BBNJ), also known as the High Seas Treaty . Oceanographic detailed how this will allow for â€œmarine protected areas across international waters for the first timeâ€, but also stressed that the â€œhard partâ€ starts now. SCOPE-FREE: The worldâ€™s largest meat supplier JBS â€œscrapped a key climate goalâ€ in its net-zero plan that accounts for its suppliersâ€™ emissions, â€œwhich make up the vast bulk of the companyâ€™s environmental footprintâ€, reported the Financial Times . The company told the paper it was difficult to control these â€œindirectâ€ emissions. DEEP TROUBLE: Pacific gray whales are facing a â€œcatastrophic die-offâ€ as sea-ice loss threatens their food sources, said the Guardian . Separately, conservationists warned that more than half of all molluscs that â€œcluster around underwater ventsâ€ could face extinction from deep-sea mining, reported Reuters . ETHANOL PUSHBACK: Indiaâ€™s new rules to promote 100% ethanol fuel and make ethanol-blended fuel mandatory at pumps â€œtriggered a political rowâ€, reported the Times of India . While the Indian government defended the push to automobile owners, a Hindu editorial and an Indian Express comment warned against incentivising fuels made from â€œwater-intensiveâ€ sugarcane and rice. AMAZON ACTION: Deforestation in the Brazilian Amazon fell to its lowest level in a decade, but president Lulaâ€™s plans to â€œend illegal deforestation by 2030â€ could be hampered if he is not re-elected, reported Al Jazeera . Meanwhile, Colombiaâ€™s outgoing environment minister warned of greater environmental and climate risk under the incoming government, said the Associated Press . WAR WORRIES: The International Energy Agency (IEA) warned of the impact of the Iran war on Africaâ€™s clean cooking efforts as disruption in the strait of Hormuz has stunted supplies and increased prices of liquefied petroleum gas (LPG), explained Climate Home News . Spotlight UK â€˜discardsâ€™ Congo rainforest funding Amid worldwide cuts to aid spending, Carbon Brief explores how the UK is backtracking on funding for the Congo basin â€“ the worldâ€™s second-largest rainforest. The UK has abandoned projects worth tens of millions of pounds that were meant to help protect Congo rainforests and support local people.&nbsp; Together, these initiatives would have made up half of the Â£200m that the UK pledged to support forest conservation in the Congo basin. When it hosted COP26 in Glasgow, the UK led a new initiative to end forest loss, which included a collective pledge of â€œat leastâ€ $1.5bn (Â£1.1bn) for Congo rainforest nations by 2025. Development minister Jenny Chapman revealed last week that, as of 2024, the UK had only provided Â£39.8m towards this goal. COP pledge At COP26, the UK â€“ led by then prime minister Boris Johnson â€“ launched the â€œ Glasgow leadersâ€™ declaration â€, with a goal to â€œhalt and reverse forest lossâ€ by 2030. The UK also made various regional funding pledges, including Â£200m for the Congo basin, Â£350m for tropical forests in Indonesia and â€œ up to Â£300m â€ for the Amazon. All of these rainforests face major forest loss . The Congo basin is the planetâ€™s largest forested carbon sink, but its six host nations are among the poorest in the world and face significant funding barriers. This has global ramifications. An official UK assessment warned that â€œdegradation or collapseâ€ of the Amazon or Congo rainforests â€œthreaten UK national security and prosperityâ€. African elephant pictured in Congo. Credit: BIOSPHOTO / Alamy Stock Photo Forest cuts Following successive aid cuts introduced by both Conservative and Labour governments â€“ tracking a global trend â€“ the UKâ€™s Congo funding is under threat. The Congo basin forest action programme (CBFA) was explicitly set up to provide â€œroughly halfâ€ of the UKâ€™s Â£200m Congo pledge. Now, after reporting delays, the UK has slashed the CBFA as part of the Labour governmentâ€™s aid cuts. Its Â£90m budget has been â€œquietly reduced by 79% to Â£18.8mâ€, according to the Times . This is not the only Congo project that has been dropped due to aid cuts. The Congo part of the biodiverse landscapes fund â€“ worth at least Â£12.3m â€“ has closed five years early. Official documents reveal more Congo forest funding is at risk, including the UKâ€™s two largest remaining projects in the region. One initiative , intended to â€œincubate forest-friendly enterprisesâ€ in DRC, faces â€œreduc[ed] budgetsâ€. Documents also show the difficulties operating in the Congo, including â€œ complex political economies â€ and, in Gabon, a military coup â€“ which â€œ complicated matters â€. â€˜Breaking promisesâ€™ Damian Fleming , a senior forests director at WWF International told Carbon Brief: â€œTropical forest countries are making long-term policy and development choices in expectation that international partners will honour their commitments.â€ In a parliamentary response , Chapman said that the UK had spent Â£39.8m towards its Â£200m Congo target, as of 2024.&nbsp; Despite being described as the UKâ€™s contribution to the Â£1.1bn-by-2025 global goal agreed at COP26, the Â£200m target has a deadline of 2029. Therefore, while the collective goal has been met, the UKâ€™s contribution was relatively small . Zac Goldsmith , a former Conservative minister who oversaw the forest targets at COP26, told Carbon Brief that, in his view, the UK has â€œdiscardedâ€ its regional pledges: â€œWe have gone from being perhaps the leader on protecting nature internationally to breaking promises to countries around the world.â€ The Labour government says it has met its overarching â€œclimate financeâ€ goals and still intends to â€œ prioritise â€ the Congo rainforest.&nbsp; However, civil society groups and MPs are concerned about the lack of â€œring-fencedâ€ forest funding in the UKâ€™s new aid strategy. Watch, read, listen TOXIC TROUBLES: DeSmog unpacked a new report that said Northern Ireland is being turned into a â€œtoxicâ€ pig and poultry farming â€œsacrifice zoneâ€ to satiate the UKâ€™s meat appetite. NEED TO NOAA: Laid-off scientists from the USâ€™s National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration (NOAA) launched Climate.Us â€“ an independent, public-backed version of the climate information website shut down by Trump last year. DRY FRUIT: A Dialogue Earth long read looked at how climate change is impacting apricot harvests in the â€œstark, high-altitude desertâ€ region of Ladakh, India.&nbsp; READING ALOUD: A London Review of Books podcast discussed Robin Wall Kimmererâ€™s influential book â€œBraiding Sweetgrassâ€, weighing its compelling themes and where it veers into â€œscientific overreachâ€.&nbsp; New science Climate change could cause Indigenous peoples in the Amazon to lose 28-34% of their plant species and 18-23% of their associated services | Nature Biodiversity in forests can act as a â€œbufferâ€ against compound extreme weather events | Nature Communications Zero-deforestation commitments in Indonesiaâ€™s palm oil sector have had â€œno additional impactsâ€ on reducing forest loss | Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences In the diary 7-15 July: High-level political forum on sustainable development | New York City 13-31 July: Meeting of the International Seabed Authority assembly and council | Kingston, Jamaica 16 July: International Energy Agency critical minerals outlook 2026 , online 27 July-1 August: Scientific and technical subsidiary body meeting of the UN Convention on Biological Diversity | Nairobi, Kenya This edition of Cropped was written by Jess Milligan , Josh Gabbatiss and Aruna Chandrasekhar . Cropped is edited by Dr Giuliana Viglione. This edition was edited by Daisy Dunne. Please send tips and feedback to cropped@carbonbrief.org . Cropped 1 July 2026: Heatwave scorches Europe | UK 2050 farm plan | Whatâ€™s next for the High Seas Treaty Cropped | 01.07.26 Cropped 17 June 2026: Coral reef â€˜hopeâ€™ | Ocean talks | Plant flowering times â€˜shiftâ€™ Cropped | 17.06.26 Cropped 3 June 2026: Highway through the Amazon | El NiÃ±o impact | State of CO2 removal Cropped | 03.06.26 Cropped 20 May 2026: Deforestation roadmap | Melanesian Ocean Summit | Returning pet parrots to the wild Cropped | 20.05.26 jQuery(document).ready(function() { jQuery('.block-related-articles-slider-block_889775003838d0a80dc0b04bb579d733 .mh').matchHeight({ byRow: false }); }); The post Cropped 15 July 2026: Uganda starves | Trump opens endangered habitats | UK cuts rainforest aid appeared first on Carbon Brief .ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;JHiVkiuygZv-J9STW5_g-u0iUrghfNPGZMH_d-TMJwsƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx_Cropped 15 July 2026: Uganda starves | Trump opens endangered habitats | UK cuts rainforest aidƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;8dglWD6